Zooms the plot into the main data cluster by applying
\code{\link[ggplot2:coord_cartesian]{ggplot2::coord_cartesian()}}, then adds a small arrow with a value label
at the panel edge for each outlier data point. Outliers are auto-detected
using the same ratio-based approach as \code{\link[=break_outlier_axis]{break_outlier_axis()}}: a gap
between consecutive sorted positive values is declared when their ratio
exceeds \code{cutoff}. The segment with the most unique values becomes the
"main cluster" (zoom region); everything outside it is annotated.

Arrows for high y-outliers point upward at the top of the panel; low
y-outliers point downward at the bottom. The mirror logic applies to
x-axis outliers. A \code{margin} fraction is added beyond the cluster boundary
so data at the edge is not clipped. Plot margins are automatically enlarged
to prevent arrows and labels that extend beyond the panel from being cut by
the device boundary.
}
\section{Limitations}{

\itemize{
\item Overrides any existing \verb{coord_*} on the plot.
\item Outlier detection uses positive values only; negative-value outliers
are not detected.
\item Works best with linear scales.
\item When multiple outlier points share the same position on the opposite
axis, one arrow is drawn per unique position showing the extreme value.
